<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zapatista Army of National Liberation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EZLN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zapatista Army of National Liberation (EZLN) is a mainly indigenous revolutionary movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, named after the revolutionary Emiliano Zapata. On 1 January 1994, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-american-free-trade-agreement">NAFTA</a> came into force, it seized several towns in the state, demanding land, rights and autonomy for indigenous peoples. After a short fight and a ceasefire, it turned to politics and built self-governing communities, with Subcomandante Marcos as its best-known spokesman.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zapatista-army-of-national-liberation.jpg" alt="Zapatista Army of National Liberation" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zscout370 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ej%C3%A9rcito_Zapatista_de_Liberaci%C3%B3n_Nacional,_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
